+      "decision": "Naive is the symbolic system, and it runs agents. The glossary gives each word with the source it is taken from. The context cites the figures the runtime choices rest on. The decisions after that are Torian's, settled 2026-11-02. This record builds on EE-105 (accounts, organizations and ventures, draft pull request 161). EE-105 is not on framework. It makes the organization the tenant and keeps every organization in one database. Decisions 9, 10 and 14 keep those choices.\n\nContext\n\nTwo research notes prepared for Torian on 2026-11-02 are the background. symbolic-system-ontology.md ranks analogs. The closest is a governed knowledge graph of sourced statements, in the shape of Wikibase, nanopublications and the Palantir Ontology (https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Wikibase/DataModel/Primer, https://nanopub.net/guidelines/working_draft/, https://www.palantir.com/docs/foundry/ontology/overview/). Next is a system of record under systems of engagement, from Geoffrey Moore's 2011 AIIM paper (https://info.aiim.org/hs-fs/file-2314261840-pdf/Inbound_Assets/Systems-of-Engagement.pdf). agent-runtime.md ranks runtime patterns. The first is the record as the control plane, a durable workflow as the run, and a sandbox for untrusted work: Vercel Workflow \"runs multi-step logic as durable code that can retry failed steps, wait for external events, and resume across crashes and deployments\" (https://vercel.com/docs/workflow), and Vercel Sandbox runs code \"in isolated Linux microVMs\" (https://vercel.com/docs/vercel-sandbox). Vendor runtimes (AWS Bedrock AgentCore, Vertex AI Agent Engine, Anthropic Managed Agents) come second, as later targets behind an adapter. Cursor Cloud Agents come third, for code work (https://cursor.com/docs/cloud-agent). Where a note says \"Naive reading\", that line is the note's interpretation. The notes recommended that Naive only store and serve agent configuration, and that it leave out task tracking and chat history. Decisions 5 and 12 go the other way.\n\nWorkflow pricing below is Vercel doc text in search results, as the research notes record it, from https://vercel.com/docs/workflows/pricing: \"$0.02 per 1K events\", \"$0.50 per GB\" written, \"$0.50 per GB-month\" retained, and \"a normal step function execution produces three events\".\n\nAI Gateway budget details below are Vercel doc text in search results, as the research notes record them. The spend page says: \"Give your team, a single project, or an individual API key a budget... AI Gateway checks the budget before each request and stops further requests once the limit is reached\" (https://vercel.com/docs/ai-gateway/observability-and-spend). Requests can carry user and tags, and the reporting API can group_by user or tag (Pro and Enterprise) (https://vercel.com/docs/ai-gateway/observability-and-spend/custom-reporting). Gateway user budgets mean members of the Vercel team, not Naive's customers (https://vercel.com/docs/ai-gateway/observability-and-spend/budgets).\n\nThe other figures are quoted by the same notes from the pages they name. The retention page keeps Workflow run payloads only \"7 days after the run finishes\" on Pro (https://vercel.com/docs/workflows/retention). The AI Gateway pricing page says \"no markup and no platform fee on tokens. You pay the provider's list price\" (https://vercel.com/docs/ai-gateway/pricing). Anthropic's hosting doc says \"Anthropic token cost typically dominates container infrastructure cost by an order of magnitude or more. A minimally provisioned container runs roughly $0.05 per hour, while a single long agent session can spend dollars in tokens\" (https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/agent-sdk/hosting). The function limits page, as the notes quote it, gives Pro functions a 300 second default and an 800 second maximum (https://vercel.com/docs/functions/limitations). The sandbox pricing page, as the notes quote it, gives a max session duration of 24 hours (https://vercel.com/docs/vercel-sandbox/pricing). The notes' risks list also states that Pro functions max out at 800 seconds (1800 seconds in beta).\n\nGlossary\n\nSystem of record. \"the authoritative data source for a given data element or piece of information\" (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_of_record). Systems of engagement overlay and complement it (Moore, AIIM, link above). In this record, Naive is the system of record for an organization's knowledge and agent configuration. Chats, IDEs and agent runtimes are the systems of engagement.\n\nStatement. Wikibase stores statements, each a claim (property and value) with qualifiers, references and a rank (Wikibase data model primer, link above). A nanopublication \"consists of an assertion, the provenance of the assertion... and the provenance of the whole nanopublication\" (nanopublication guidelines, link above). Here the basic unit is a sourced statement. \"Claim\" is a status of a statement.\n\nSource. The document or other origin a statement cites. Documents are kept so statements can cite them. An outside source is referenced. Naive stores the statements extracted from it, with links back.\n\nJudgment. An assessment of a statement by a named agent using a named method. It is its own record type, linked to the statement it assesses.\n\nAgent configuration. The instructions, model, capabilities, declared reads, role and owner for one agent. MLflow prompt versions \"are immutable\", with aliases (https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/genai/prompt-registry/). An A2A Agent Card is \"A JSON metadata document published by an A2A Server, describing its identity, capabilities, skills, service endpoint, and authentication requirements\" (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/a2aproject/A2A/main/docs/specification.md). Here a configuration is stored as fixed versions with named aliases.\n\nPolicy. \"OPA decouples policy decision-making from policy enforcement. When your software needs to make policy decisions it queries OPA and supplies structured data (e.g., JSON) as input\" (https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs). Declared reads, gate rules and access are policies, stored as data and checked by one policy engine.\n\nMCP. A server exposes \"Prompts: Pre-defined templates or instructions\", \"Resources: Structured data or content\" and \"Tools: Executable functions\" (https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/server). MCP is the main interface. A REST API sits beside it.\n\nSKOS. \"a common data model for sharing and linking knowledge organization systems... thesauri, taxonomies, classification schemes\" (https://www.w3.org/TR/skos-reference/). Each organization can add its own terms on top of the framework vocabulary.\n\nRDF. \"sets of subject-predicate-object triples\" (https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf11-concepts/). The graph is stored in Postgres. An RDF/JSON-LD export is the portable form. The research notes name that export shape. They cite RDF 1.1 for the triples.\n\nRun. One execution of an agent. The Run record names the agent, the configuration version, the acting person and the organization, and at the end holds the trace summary. PROV-O: an activity \"acts upon or with entities\", and an agent \"bears some form of responsibility for an activity\" (https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-o/). The notes use actedOnBehalfOf for the person the run acts for.\n\nSandbox. An isolated microVM for one run. \"The credential never enters the microVM\" (https://vercel.com/blog/a-sandbox-without-a-network-boundary-is-only-half-a-sandbox). One sandbox per run, never reused across organizations.\n\nDecisions\n\nSymbolic system.\n\n1. Naive is the system of record: a governed knowledge graph of an organization's knowledge and agent configuration. Chats, IDEs and agent runtimes are systems of engagement on top of it.\n\n2. The basic unit is a sourced statement. Documents are kept as the sources statements cite.\n\n3. History is append-only, with a separate redaction path for legally required deletion of personal data.\n\n4. An agent configuration holds instructions, model, capabilities, declared reads, role and owner, stored as fixed versions with named aliases.\n\n5. Naive also runs agents, as a deliberate strategic bet. The runtime decisions are 14 to 25.\n\n6. MCP is the main interface, with a REST API beside it.\n\n7. Policies (declared reads, gate rules, access) are stored as data and checked by one policy engine.\n\n8. A judgment is its own record type, linked to the statement it assesses.\n\n9. The graph is stored in Postgres with an RDF/JSON-LD export, keeping the one-database decision (EE-105).\n\n10. Each organization can extend the vocabulary with its own SKOS terms.\n\n11. Outside sources are referenced, not copied. Naive stores statements extracted from them with links back.\n\n12. Beyond knowledge and agent configuration, Naive also holds tasks and chat history.\n\n13. No branching and merging of knowledge yet. Draft and accepted statement statuses are enough for now.\n\nAgent runtime.\n\n14. The first agents do knowledge work: research, Jev scoring and drafting statements. Code work goes to Cursor cloud agents, and code stays in GitHub.\n\n15. The runtime is built on Vercel Workflow, the AI SDK, Sandbox and AI Gateway.\n\n16. Every run is a Run record, with its trace summary copied in at the end. Vercel keeps workflow run data readable only 7 days on Pro. The retention page, as the notes quote it, keeps run payloads \"7 days after the run finishes\" (link above).\n\n17. Agents only propose statements. A gate or a person accepts them, so people and agents share one write path.\n\n18. Triggers: manual and scheduled first, then changes in the record, then outside webhooks.\n\n19. Each organization gets its own AI Gateway API key, budget and tags.\n\n20. Credentials stay out of agents entirely, injected at the sandbox edge, with per-person OAuth tokens in a vault keyed by agent and person.\n\n21. A person must approve anything that leaves Naive (send, post, merge, purchase) and any accept that changes a gate outcome.\n\n22. Default to the AI SDK agent loop. Use the Claude Agent SDK inside a sandbox only for shell or computer tasks.\n\n23. Each organization pays its metered token cost at list price first, with the option to bring its own key later. The pricing page quote is above: list price, no markup and no platform fee on tokens.\n\n24. One sandbox per run, never reused across organizations.\n\n25. The runtime sits behind one adapter so it can move to a vendor later, for example AWS Bedrock AgentCore (https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/what-is-bedrock-agentcore.html) or Anthropic Managed Agents (https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/agent-sdk/hosting).\n\nRisks\n\nThe runtime notes name these risks of the bet. Decisions 16, 19, 20, 21, 24 and 25 take up several of the mitigations the notes name. This record does not build them.\n\n1. Cost exposure. Token cost dominates container cost, as the Anthropic hosting doc states (roughly $0.05 per hour for a minimal container, and dollars in tokens for a long session). A runaway loop spends money before anyone looks. The notes' mitigation: organization key budgets, per-run step and token caps, and stop on budget.\n\n2. Prompt injection with real permissions. Running agents means acting with people's credentials. Vercel's changelog says brokered credentials leave \"nothing to exfiltrate\" (https://vercel.com/changelog/safely-inject-credentials-in-http-headers-with-vercel-sandbox). The notes' mitigation: brokering, deny-by-default egress, and approvals for side effects.\n\n3. Cross-tenant leakage. Anthropic's hosting doc warns that in a shared container, settings and memory files \"can leak one tenant's context into another tenant's session\" (https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/agent-sdk/hosting). The notes' mitigation: one sandbox per run, scoped reads, and no shared agent memory.\n\n4. Record drift. Vercel keeps Workflow run payloads 7 days on Pro (retention page, above). If the trace summary is not copied into the Run record at the end, the record is incomplete.\n\n5. Liability for acting on behalf of people. The notes cite AgentCore, which binds each token to a user with explicit consent \"from user authentication through to agent action\" (https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/secure-ai-agents-with-amazon-bedrock-agentcore-identity-on-amazon-ecs/).\n\n6. Operating burden and focus. Running agents is a product of its own. AgentCore, Vertex AI Agent Engine and Anthropic Managed Agents are whole platforms. The notes say this can pull the team away from the record and the gates.\n\n7. Platform coupling. Workflow, Sandbox and Gateway are Vercel products. The notes' mitigation is the adapter in decision 25. The Workflow pricing page, as the notes cite it, also lists a Postgres World for where run state lives (https://vercel.com/docs/workflows/pricing).\n\n8. Limits. The notes' risks list states that Pro functions max out at 800 seconds (1800 seconds in beta) and sandboxes at 24 hours per session. The quoted pages give a 300 second default and an 800 second maximum for Pro functions, and 24 hours for a sandbox session (links above). Long runs have to be split into durable steps.\n\nConflicts and follow-ups\n\nChecked against framework at cb502f9. This pull request does not change code, schemas or earlier entries. EE-105 stays on its own branch. A later change can rewrite an earlier entry's status. This one does not.\n\n1. Naive is driven by whichever agents the founder already has, and the framework names no vendor. EE-098 (in force, docs/decisions/decisions.json) says Naive \"acts the same whichever agents drive it.\" EE-041 (proposed, same file) puts hosting and vendor calls on the platform: the framework works offline, with no account and without naming a vendor. schema/check-boundary.mjs enforces that split: platform code lives in platform/, nothing outside it imports from platform/, and a framework connector uses no network and names no credentials. interface/harness/run.mjs says the agent is Claude Code, headless, and \"Naive holds no model key.\" Decisions 5 and 15 say Naive runs agents on Vercel Workflow, the AI SDK, Sandbox and AI Gateway. That names vendors and needs the network.\n\n2. The unit on disk is a JSON record file. schema/data-model.json lists record types as files. There is no statement record type. EE-033 (in force) chooses JSON when a program reads the file. schema/venture-core.mjs statementsIn names foundation fields on venture.json (promise, competence, tagline). That is a different use of the word statement. schema/raw-source.schema.json keeps a founder-supplied source at raw/sources/<id>/source.json and says text.md is the text, so the source text is stored in the venture. Decision 2 makes a sourced statement the unit, and decision 11 keeps outside sources as references with extracted statements and links back.\n\n3. A write replaces the file, and the log cannot drop a line. knowledge/connectors/filesystem.mjs is the default source (EE-037) and its write replaces the file (writes default to direct). EE-031 (in force) and schema/provenance-entry.schema.json keep history in provenance.jsonl: one append-only line per write, a hash of the content, and records carry no history of their own. schema/check-provenance.mjs fails when a line was edited, removed or reordered. Decision 3 keeps history append-only and adds a separate redaction path for legally required deletion of personal data. No such path exists.\n\n4. Agent instructions are the live AGENTS.md chain. EE-073 (proposed) says AGENTS.md is the file agents read. interface/router.mjs assembles that chain from the venture root down to the route. schema/agent-capability.schema.json (EE-029) is a register of what agents can do, task by task, at evidence/capabilities/agent-capabilities.json. No record stores instructions, model, capabilities, declared reads, role and owner as fixed versions with named aliases (decision 4).\n\n5. An agent write can be the record, and people and agents do not share one write path. interface/router.mjs gateOf returns null when the record type has no decision_class, and the write is then a draft only when a gate rule or a holding decision says so. On this commit, schema/data-model.json has 128 record types and 106 of them name no decision_class, including customer, handoff, judgment and raw-source. packages/customers/skill/SKILL.md says \"Customer records have no escalating class, so an agent's write is the record.\" interface/mcp/server.mjs writes every caller as an agent. interface/api/server.mjs does the same (actor agent). A person's direct write is the CLI (interface/cli/mentura.mjs, actor person). Decision 17 says agents only propose, and people and agents share one write path.\n\n6. Accept is recorded as a person even when an agent runs the command. interface/cli/mentura.mjs accept sets actor to person, and its comment says the CLI cannot tell a person from an agent running it. Decision 21 requires a person for any accept that changes a gate outcome, and for anything that leaves Naive (send, post, merge, purchase). EE-025 (in force) already requires a person to approve publication. governance/venture-template/decision-rights.json marks spend-money as escalate. The accept command is the hole.\n\n7. Policy is several checks, and a role with no routes may read every route. schema/gate.mjs is one gate per venture for writes: rules from naive.config.json, package gate_rules, and two framework rules. schema/access.mjs may() returns true when the role lists no routes (globsOf returns null). schema/visibility.mjs is a separate check for what may be published. interface/acting.mjs refuses an agent that acts in a role whose occupant is person, so an agent does not act on behalf of a named person in that person's role. Decision 4 puts role and owner on the agent configuration. Decision 7 stores declared reads, gate rules and access as data and checks them in one policy engine. Declared reads are not a stored policy on an agent.\n\n8. A judgment links to an item path. schema/judgment.schema.json is the output of one model step (\"use model\", EE-073): field item is what it was asked about, and schema/model-steps.mjs writes it through the router. Decision 8 keeps judgment as its own record type and links it to the statement it assesses. The type exists. The statement link does not.\n\n9. The store is a folder per venture, not one Postgres database. knowledge/connectors/filesystem.mjs holds the records. EE-086 and schema/venture-paths.mjs put the knowledge base in knowledge/ inside the venture folder. Nothing writes an RDF or JSON-LD export. Decision 9 stores the graph in Postgres and keeps EE-105's one database. EE-105 is not on framework. platform/research/store.mjs comments that research cells could later use Neon Postgres, one branch per research run. That comment is not this store, and a branch per run disagrees with decision 13.\n\n10. Code can live inside the venture, and Naive can write file contents to GitHub. schema/port-venture.mjs moves a folder into codebase/ (schema/venture-paths.mjs, CODEBASE) and names it a filesystem source of truth. EE-086 also allows repo/ for code. platform/connectors/github.mjs writes file contents by opening a branch and a draft pull request. Decision 14 sends code work to Cursor cloud agents and keeps code in GitHub. operations/tooling/integrations.json lists Cursor background agents as in-use for handoffs (EE-006). That entry is not a dispatch of code work to Cursor cloud agents.\n\n11. Knowledge stored in GitHub is branched and merged. EE-037 (proposed) and platform/connectors/github.mjs: a write opens a draft pull request, and merging it accepts the write. Decision 13 says no branching and merging of knowledge yet. Draft and accepted do exist for some records: escalating classes become drafts in interface/router.mjs, and schema/gate-drafts.mjs holds them. Decision records have their own statuses (EE-054). Those are not statement statuses, and the GitHub path is still branch and merge.\n\n12. Three interfaces, none of them primary. EE-030 (in force) puts a CLI (interface/cli/mentura.mjs), an MCP server (interface/mcp/server.mjs) and a local HTTP API (interface/api/server.mjs) over interface/router.mjs. Decision 6 makes MCP the main interface, with a REST API beside it.\n\n13. Triggers already fire on record changes. schema/triggers.mjs treats a provenance line (a record written, drafted, accepted, confirmed or moved) as the event, and the trigger starts the next router call. Decision 18 orders agent-run triggers as manual and scheduled first, then changes in the record, then outside webhooks. The runner that exists is the record-change kind.\n\n14. The running agent is an outside harness, and the Claude Agent SDK is not limited to shell or computer tasks. interface/tooling/integrations.json lists Claude Code and Cursor as in-use. operations/tooling/integrations.json lists the Claude Agent SDK as a candidate whose role is \"build and run Claude agents in code\". EE-096 (in force) defines harness with the Agent SDK, Claude Managed Agents and Claude Code. interface/harness/run.mjs spawns claude with the process environment (env spreads process.env), so keys in the environment enter the agent process, and the research server is given FIRECRAWL_API_KEY. Decision 20 keeps credentials out of agents, injected at the sandbox edge. Decision 22 defaults to the AI SDK agent loop and uses the Claude Agent SDK inside a sandbox only for shell or computer tasks.\n\n15. The existing adapter runs Naive on a harness. EE-073 defines adapter as what runs Naive on a platform or harness. interface/adapters.mjs writes the AGENTS.md entry and skills a harness reads. Decision 25 puts the runtime behind one adapter so execution can move to a vendor. That is the other direction.\n\n16. The work unit is a handoff, and there is no chat history. EE-038 (proposed) says a handoff is how operational work is assigned. schema/handoff.schema.json is that record, under operations/handoffs. schema/data-model.json has no task record and no chat-history record. Decision 12 says Naive also holds tasks and chat history.\n\n17. The vocabulary is one framework file. docs/ontology.json is canonical for nouns (EE-014, in force). schema/check-nouns.mjs fails when a retired noun reappears in the files it checks. It does not store an organization's own terms. Decision 10 lets each organization extend the vocabulary with its own SKOS terms. No concept scheme for an organization exists.\n",
